92186045417] [17592186199259 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6144c3d-16c1-5ac5-841d-01a96fe71c2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199259 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199260 #edb/ref 1061 30628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199260 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "38ffe571-8650-5b48-a6db-d2d7374b7548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199260 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1061 44879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6197786-2f72-519b-bd74-410c8f09a532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199329 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1061 44905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a2f3763-0f5f-5328-a240-5a9328c979b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199330 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a lamina's *center of mass* is the point $(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})$ where its total mass could be concentrated to give the same total moment about the coordinate axes as the original lamina. \n\nThe center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x},\\overline{y}) = \\left( \\dfrac{M_y}{m}, \\dfrac{M_x}{m} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\iint \\limits_D \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_x = \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\\:$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_y = \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\lambda (x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nRemember that if we could place a fulcrum under our lamina at its center of mass, it would balance perfectly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1065 4905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbc92201-9cb2-54d6-9ac9-8ee49bf8c4e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199454 #edb/ref 1067 "Center of Mass" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin lamina whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution across a lamina using its mass density function $\\lambda (x,y).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit area* at any point $(x,y)$ on the lamina.\n\nFor example, consider a thin plate with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y) = 6xy$ occupying the finite rectangular region $D$ between the lines $x=1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=1.$ What is the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $(x,y) \\in D$ on the plate and a small rectangular piece with its bottom-left corner at $(x,y)$ and area $\\Delta{A},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8cd947e542aaf7eb787abc7a846e87a2d03ccafa0987f3422928fdcba115da9e.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{A}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the moment of this piece about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{y} & ≈x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & ≈x\\cdot 6xy\\,ΔA \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y\\,ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total moment of the plate about the axis, we add together the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta{A} \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nM_y =  \\iint\\limits_D 6x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the moment of the plate about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe moment about the $x$-axis can be found similarly.\n\nIn general, the moments $M_x$ and $M_y$ of a lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ about the $x$-axis and about the $y$-axis, respectively, are given by the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1065 4906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7aa18cfe-acd7-5e17-982c-58eabc063404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199455 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1064 "When a lamina's mass density is constant, the center of mass depends only on the shape of the lamina. In cases like these, the center of mass is called the **centroid** of the lamina. The centroid of a shape corresponds to its geometrical center.\n\nSo, $\\lambda$ is constant, then the mass of the plate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda \\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda {∬}_{D}\\,dA \\\\  & =\\lambda \\cdot {A}_{D},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A_D$ denotes the area of $D.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nm = \\lambda \\cdot A_D \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\dfrac{\\lambda}{m} = \\dfrac{1}{A_D}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the formula for the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m} & =\\frac{1}{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda \\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{\\lambda }{m}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{A}_{D}}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can similarly compute the $y$-coordinate.\n\nIn general, the centroid of a lamina $D$ with area $A_D$ is given by $(x_c, y_c),$ where\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle x_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D x \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A, \\qquad \n\\displaystyle y_c = \\dfrac{1}{A_D} \\iint\\limits_D y \\, \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1065 4907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aea964ff-901c-5b76-9fca-721d53f04354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199456 #edb/ref 1067 "Centroids" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278790 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278790 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278790 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "167e6409-5e3e-5efb-a712-07b360471d4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278940 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278940 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278940 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2732ae5b-753f-525a-b5e9-1120f08e3c31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279305 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4e521e4f-0860-5a65-9ac2-eda32bfca8ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279318 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{11}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279318 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279318 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ff0cfbd-f9da-5245-a475-255ea08605cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279658 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279658 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279658 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "775abca6-570c-5895-b34d-8941c17f178b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279814 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279814 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279814 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "88d3bab5-4d08-59c8-a45a-f21f13e573d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279873 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279873 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186279873 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8fdb2853-701b-5307-ad7e-d512948651d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280108 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{9}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280108 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280108 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ab21d0b4-eff6-548f-b2b3-7f4952880828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280726 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280726 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280726 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3d35eb7-48e3-5f6c-b3b0-c9bb57e1a8a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280809 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280809 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186280809 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd58d3b2-06c6-5e92-89a3-b6bb6ae53691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186279305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281176 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c47ab8ca-3bde-5fba-a4a7-31651f3af0b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186278940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281226 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e17aea6f-d602-5f51-a2f7-34b15c79c4b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281560 #edb/ref 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,1\\le x\\le {e}^{y}}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b4076e3-ab6e-5bdd-85c0-2781a4c652d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281560 #edb/ref 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x={e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=1}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(9-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281587 #edb/ref 1071 "The lamina $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}$ has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=5xy.$ Find the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fe9f4bda-1254-5b20-b5e7-335406a2dc03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281587 #edb/ref 1074 "The moment of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $D$ is represented as a type II plane region and $\\lambda (x,y)=5xy.$ \n\nTherefore, the moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot 5xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3y}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5y{\\int }_{0}^{3y}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5y{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=3y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}5y(9{y}^{3}-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}45{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[9{y}^{5}]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429353 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429353 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 4166->2025. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429353 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049267 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429354 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429354 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/multivariable-frequency-estimates.json, pair 4166->4167. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429354 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186049333 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
